# Core Components

Microsoft Sentinel consists of several integrated components that support the complete SOC lifecycle.

---

## Data Connectors

Data Connectors import telemetry into Sentinel.

Supported sources include:

- Azure Resources
- Microsoft Defender XDR
- Microsoft Entra ID
- Office 365
- Azure Firewall
- Windows Security Events
- Linux Syslog
- AWS CloudTrail
- Google Cloud
- Cisco
- Palo Alto Networks
- Fortinet
- Custom REST APIs

A well-designed SIEM deployment begins with selecting appropriate data connectors.

---

## Log Analytics Workspace

Microsoft Sentinel stores all telemetry inside Azure Log Analytics Workspace.

The workspace provides:

- Centralized storage
- Fast indexing
- KQL query engine
- Long-term retention
- Historical investigations

Every Sentinel capability depends on data stored within the workspace.

---

## Analytics Rules

Analytics Rules convert KQL queries into security detections.

Rules evaluate incoming telemetry and generate alerts when suspicious behavior matches predefined conditions.

Example detections:

- Brute-force authentication
- Impossible travel
- Privilege escalation
- Malware execution
- Multiple failed logins
- Suspicious PowerShell activity

Detection engineering focuses on designing effective analytics rules while minimizing false positives.

---

## Incidents

Alerts generated by Analytics Rules are grouped into incidents.

An incident represents a security event requiring analyst investigation.

Incident details typically include:

- Severity
- Entities
- Related alerts
- Timeline
- MITRE ATT&CK techniques
- Investigation graph

Grouping related alerts reduces analyst workload and provides a clearer understanding of attacker activity.

---

## Hunting

Threat Hunting enables analysts to proactively search for malicious activity before alerts are generated.

Unlike Analytics Rules, hunting queries do not automatically create incidents.

Examples include:

- Searching for failed SSH logins
- Identifying unusual administrator activity
- Detecting rare process executions
- Investigating suspicious IP addresses
- Reviewing lateral movement

Threat hunting assumes an attacker may already be present within the environment.

---

## Workbooks

Workbooks provide interactive dashboards for visualizing security data.

Common dashboard content includes:

- Authentication trends
- Geographic login activity
- Alert distribution
- Incident status
- Top affected devices
- Threat intelligence indicators
- MITRE ATT&CK coverage

Workbooks help analysts identify trends that may not be obvious from individual alerts.

---

## Watchlists

Watchlists allow organizations to enrich detections using custom datasets.

Examples:

- VIP users
- High-value assets
- Known malicious IP addresses
- Corporate IP ranges
- Service accounts
- Third-party vendors

Watchlists improve detection accuracy and reduce false positives.

---

## Automation Rules

Automation Rules execute predefined actions when incidents occur.

Examples include:

- Assign incident ownership
- Change incident severity
- Add incident tags
- Trigger playbooks
- Close duplicate incidents

Automation reduces repetitive administrative work for analysts.

---

## Playbooks

Playbooks are automated workflows built using Azure Logic Apps.

Common response actions include:

- Block malicious IP addresses
- Disable user accounts
- Notify security teams
- Create ServiceNow tickets
- Send Microsoft Teams notifications
- Update incident status

Playbooks reduce response time and improve operational consistency.

---

## Threat Intelligence

Microsoft Sentinel supports importing external threat intelligence.

Threat Intelligence may include:

- Malicious IP addresses
- File hashes
- Domains
- URLs
- Email addresses

Sentinel automatically correlates threat intelligence indicators with collected telemetry.

---

## User and Entity Behavior Analytics (UEBA)

UEBA uses machine learning to establish behavioral baselines.

Examples include:

- Unusual login locations
- Rare administrative actions
- Abnormal authentication patterns
- Privilege abuse

Behavioral analytics helps identify attacks that signature-based detection may miss.

---

## Fusion Detection

Fusion combines alerts from multiple sources into higher-confidence incidents.

Instead of investigating isolated alerts, Fusion identifies relationships between events.

Examples include:

- Phishing email
- Credential theft
- Suspicious login
- Privilege escalation

Together, these activities may indicate a coordinated attack.

